<!--
  local_rules.xml - MutaSpace SOC Lab custom detection rules

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  ====================
  Wazuh ships thousands of rules. This file holds the ones this lab wrote itself, and it is version
  controlled for the same reason application code is: a detection is a piece of logic with a
  lifecycle. It gets written, tuned, sometimes wrong, and occasionally reverted. A rule that only
  exists inside a running manager cannot be reviewed, diffed, or rebuilt.

  70-detections.yml pushes this file to the manager through the Wazuh API and then reloads
  analysisd. It is never edited on the manager by hand - an edit there is lost on the next push.

  RULE ID RANGE
  =============
  Every rule here uses an ID between 100000 and 120000. That range is reserved by Wazuh for user
  rules and is guaranteed never to be used by a shipped ruleset. A custom rule outside it works
  until the day a Wazuh update claims the same number, and then it stops working in a way that is
  extremely hard to trace.

  FIELD NAMES
  ===========
  Inside a rule, Windows fields are addressed as `win.system.eventID` and
  `win.eventdata.targetUserName`. In the dashboard's search bar the same fields carry a `data.`
  prefix - `data.win.system.eventID` - because that is the indexer's view of the document. The two
  are the same data seen from two places, and mixing them up is the most common reason a rule that
  "should obviously match" never fires.

  LEVELS
  ======
  Wazuh levels are a 0-15 scale. A single failed logon in a teaching lab is not an incident, so it
  sits at 5 - visible in Security Events, not worth alerting on. The correlated version of the same
  activity is level 10, which is where an analyst should start paying attention.
-->

<!--
  A SINGLE top-level <group> wraps every rule in this file.

  Wazuh's parser tolerates several sibling <group> blocks in one file, but that is not well-formed
  XML - a document has one root - and it means "xmllint" cannot be used to check this file
  before pushing it to a live manager. One root keeps the file checkable with ordinary tools, and
  each rule declares its own categories with its own inner <group> tag anyway.
-->
<group name="mutaspace,windows,">

  <!--
    100010 - a single Windows logon failure.

    This is the event docs/incident-scenarios/failed-login-investigation.md is built on. The
    scenario has the learner search for event ID 4625 on win-client-01; this rule gives that search
    a named, described result instead of a raw event.

    It matches on the event ID field rather than chaining off a built-in rule with <if_sid>,
    because built-in rule IDs move between ruleset versions and a chained rule that silently stops
    matching is worse than no rule at all.
  -->
  <rule id="100010" level="5">
    <if_group>windows</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4625$</field>
    <description>MutaSpace: Windows logon failure for $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) on $(win.system.computer)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failed,pci_dss_10.2.4,gdpr_IV_35.7.d,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100011 - the same failures, correlated.

    One failed logon is a typo. Five for the same account inside two minutes is a pattern, and the
    difference between those two statements is the entire job of a correlation rule.

    <same_field> is what makes this specific: without it, five failures spread across five different
    accounts would trigger the rule, which is a different behaviour (spraying) that deserves its own
    rule rather than being confused with guessing a single password.

    HONEST CAVEAT ON `frequency`: Wazuh counts matches within `timeframe`, and the exact threshold
    at which the rule fires has differed between versions - some behave as "frequency" and some as
    "frequency + 1". Before teaching a precise number to a class, generate failures on
    win-client-01 and count what it actually takes to fire. Do not present the value below as
    verified; it has not been tested against a running manager.
  -->
  <rule id="100011" level="10" frequency="5" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100010</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.eventdata.targetUserName</same_field>
    <description>MutaSpace: repeated logon failures for the same account - possible password guessing</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,pci_dss_11.4,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100020 - an Active Directory user account was created.

    This is the event docs/incident-scenarios/account-creation-investigation.md is built on. The
    scenario creates lab.user02 on dc-01 and has the learner find event ID 4720.

    Level 8 rather than 5: account creation is not inherently suspicious, but it is always worth
    explaining. The teaching point in that scenario is exactly this - "not every new account is
    suspicious, but every unexpected account creation should be explainable" - and the alert level
    is where that judgement gets encoded.

    Event 4720 only exists if User Account Management auditing is enabled on the domain controller.
    10-dc-promote.yml asserts that, which is what makes this rule reliable rather than occasional.
  -->
  <rule id="100020" level="8">
    <if_group>windows</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4720$</field>
    <description>MutaSpace: Active Directory user account $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) created by $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>account_changed,pci_dss_10.2.5,gdpr_IV_35.7.d,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100021 - a newly created account was added to a privileged group.

    4720 followed by 4728 (member added to a global security group) inside five minutes is a
    recognisable persistence pattern: create an account, then give it rights. Neither event alone
    justifies waking anyone; the sequence does.

    This rule is included because it demonstrates something the two existing scenarios do not - that
    detection value often lives in the relationship between events rather than in any single one.
  -->
  <rule id="100021" level="12" timeframe="300">
    <if_group>windows</if_group>
    <if_matched_sid>100020</if_matched_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4728$</field>
    <description>MutaSpace: recently created account added to a privileged group - possible persistence</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.002</id>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>account_changed,privilege_escalation,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
